{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat decisions/D025-build-to-result-chain.md cc/engine_handoff/ENVELOPES.md cc/build_engine/README.md builds/results/novmm-4/build-record.json LAB-FEASIBILITY.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat decisions/D025-build-to-result-chain.md cc/engine_handoff/ENVELOPES.md cc/build_engine/README.md builds/results/novmm-4/build-record.json LAB-FEASIBILITY.md'","aggregated_output":"# D025 — First complete x86 chain: Build CC -> lab job with input identity -> dispatchable proposal -> admission -> execution -> result envelope\nClaim category: infrastructure validation (temporary reference adapter = our own lab; NOT the other team's lab agent).\n1. Build CC v2 novmm-4 (builds/results/novmm-4): status=success in 49 s (cache), -DGGML_CUDA_NO_VMM=ON, linkage passed host-side (DT_NEEDED libcudart.so.12; cudaMalloc/cudaFree/cudaMemGetInfo undefined imports; no cuMemCreate import — the VMM build shot-2 has one, T-N3). Archive sha256 9dc43cf8..., 2210029568 bytes.\n   Identity: config_digest 0b78d876... is IDENTICAL to novmm-2's (content rebuilds reproducibly); index digest differs per build (attestations) -> config digest is the identity key; run_ref (= Docker reported ID = index digest under the containerd store) is only what local docker run accepts.\n2. Seam found between T-Q (lab stores test-definition hashes) and T-S (envelope needs definitions to bind argv): rule adopted — the job spec embeds an immutable copy of its test definitions (bin/lab cmd_submit), and the runner executes the embedded copy, not mutable lab/tests/*.json. First submission without it (job-ae5af3eaa2) stays non-dispatchable (needs-information: argv), preserved.\n3. job-61a2897cda: proposal dispatchable=true, unresolved=[]; admit x86-main -> accepted; admit jetson-arm.TEMPLATE -> rejected (platform linux/amd64 vs linux/arm64, host_id, cuda_arch, jetpack_l4t missing). Executed: 2/2 clean, states queued..terminal, export manifest present, stop_confirmed=true; result envelope artifacts/x5-env/result-chain.json (separate execution/assessment/claim/coverage/durable-sequence/resource-generation fields).\n4. Indicative only (1 sample each): novmm-4 decode n0 676 t/s, n12 215 t/s vs b0 D022 medians 684 / 197 -> no visible NO_VMM penalty; not a claim.\nNot demonstrated: Jetson acceptance (needs a real arm64 build + filled host profile), remote transport, crash during this chain.\n# Shared job envelopes (T-S)\n\n`envelopes.py` is a thin offline projection of Build CC records and `bin/lab`\njob/attempt/export records. It does not create a new executor, provision a\nsandbox, contact a receiver, inspect processes, run Docker, or use the network.\nThe other team's lab agent is not exercised by these checks.\n\n## Python and CLI contract\n\nThe three entry points are `make_proposal(build_record, job_spec, host_profile,\npolicy)`, `check_admission(proposal, host_profile)`, and\n`make_result(job_dir, attempt, claim_category=\"unclassified\", *,\nmanifest_path=None)`. They return JSON-compatible dictionaries. Inputs to the\nfirst two functions are dictionaries; `job_spec` accepts either the immutable\n`job.json` wrapper or its `spec`. No argument dictionaries are mutated.\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/envelopes.py proposal \\\n  --build-record builds/results/b0-baseline-5/build-record.json \\\n  --job-spec lab/jobs/job-ad883810b3/job.json \\\n  --host-profile cc/engine_handoff/hosts/x86-main.json \\\n  --policy cc/engine_handoff/fixtures/envelopes/policy.example.json \\\n  --out artifacts/codexS/proposal-real.json\n\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/envelopes.py admit \\\n  --proposal artifacts/codexS/proposal-real.json \\\n  --host-profile cc/engine_handoff/hosts/jetson-arm.TEMPLATE.json\n\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 cc/engine_handoff/envelopes.py result \\\n  --job-dir lab/jobs/job-8df3c7bb27 --attempt att-1 \\\n  --claim-category interrupted-observation \\\n  --out artifacts/codexS/result-interrupted.json\n```\n\nAll commands write JSON to stdout unless `--out` is supplied. `admit` returns\n0 for accepted and 2 for rejected or needs-information. Malformed files or\ninconsistent result ownership return 2 with a JSON error on stderr. Generation\nof an unresolved proposal returns 0: inspect `dispatchable` and `unresolved`.\n\nThe example policy is a **new requester declaration**, not recovered historical\npolicy. It specifies whole-job wall time and capture bytes; the original lab's\nper-test `resources.timeout_s` is deliberately not relabelled as a job budget.\nPolicy supplies `campaign_id`, `experiment_id`, `attempt_id`, `release_role`,\n`build_record_ref`, `wall_time_s`, `capture_bytes`, `memory`, and `evidence`.\n`request_id` defaults to the recorded job ID or spec key. Explicit spec IDs take\nprecedence over policy IDs. Unknown IDs are null, never inferred from filenames.\n\n## Proposal fields\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `format` | `campaign-job-proposal/0` |\n| `campaign_id`, `request_id`, `experiment_id`, `attempt_id` | Requester/controller identities |\n| `target.host_id`, `target.platform`, `target.required_capabilities` | Selected receiver and image's required platform/runtime |\n| `target.platform_compat` | `cuda_arch` from CMake, plus optional declared `jetpack_l4t`, `driver_min` from `spec.platform_compat` |\n| `release.role`, `release.source` | Release role, recorded source commit and tree |\n| `release.patch_sha256`, `release.cmake_defines` | Patch identity (null means unpatched), all parsed CMake flags plus schema 2 defines |\n| `release.transport` | `kind: docker-archive`, recorded SHA-256 and bytes |\n| `release.image_config_id`, `release.index_digest` | Separate config/index identities; unavailable index may be null |\n| `release.executables_sha256`, `release.build_record_ref` | Recorded `/app` file hashes (libraries included), record locator |\n| `inputs` | `spec.inputs` model, prompt/data, and canonical test-definition hashes |\n| `invocation.tests` | Ordered test occurrences with argv, environment, declared cache state |\n| `invocation.mounts` | Role, access, optional container target; no host bind paths |\n| `policy` | Whole-job wall/capture budgets, memory domain/budget/guarantee/accounting, `gpu_jobs_on_host: 1` |\n| `evidence` | Controller outside sandbox, durable destination reference, expected output names |\n| `unresolved`, `dispatchable` | Recomputed identity/policy/receiver gaps; dispatchable only when empty |\n\nNested build-record schemas 1 and 2 are accepted. `image.config_digest` is used\nfor config identity; Docker's reported `Id` is never substituted for it. A null\nindex digest is retained rather than fabricated from an archive catalog or an\nuntyped Docker ID. If the job's image ID is not any recorded config/reported/\nindex identity, the build/job pairing is unresolved. This occurs in the specified\nreal records: they are useful fixtures, but are not a matching dispatchable pair.\n\nThe specified jobs are also legacy submissions without `spec.inputs`. Their\n`model_sha256` is not promoted into a pretend full input manifest. Missing input\nidentities reject admission; missing transport SHA requests more information.\nOther unknown required declarations result in needs-information. A known\nplatform, CUDA architecture, driver floor, capability, or memory guarantee\nmismatch rejects admission even if other fields remain unknown. `dispatchable`\nand any sender-supplied flag are never trusted by admission.\n\nA newly pinned proposal can supply `spec.test_definitions`, using the existing\n`bin/lab` definition mapping. Definition identity is the same canonical SHA-256\nas `bin/lab`: sorted keys, compact separators, ASCII JSON. The adapter constructs\n`/app/<tool> -m /m/<model_file>`, optional `-f /d/<data_file>`, and definition args.\nIt preserves occurrence order, including repeated tests. Read-only `-v` and\n`--volume` forms become input roles. Other Docker flags stay unresolved.\n`spec.invocation` may instead explicitly declare the complete invocation.\n\nHost paths from `inputs.mounts` become `prompt-0`, `prompt-1`, etc. in sorted host\nlocator order; file manifests retain relative names and hashes. Data locators\nbecome basenames. Model/data default container targets are `/m` and `/d`; results\nis `/results`. Prompt roles retain their declared container target, e.g. `/tin`.\nAbsolute argv paths must resolve to a hashed input in these mounts (or a results\noutput). Receiver staging resolves roles to its own paths. Build-record and\ndurable-store references remain locators, not bind instructions.\n\n**Trust boundary:** proposal values are copied declarations. This adapter does\nnot re-observe archive bytes or prove model completeness, provenance, authenticity,\nor invocation safety. The existing `engine_handoff.py` archive observation and\nrequirements receipt must still bind delivered bytes before execution. An\naccepted compatibility receipt is neither execution nor test assessment. The\nrequested GPU concurrency of one is not a new GPU lock or capacity reservation.\nCooperative interception requires the right engine/shim and demonstrated runtime\ninterception (D021/D023); a host capability declaration does not prove these.\nCgroup host RAM accounting is not a discrete VRAM guarantee.\n\n## Result fields and evidence limits\n\n`campaign-job-result/0` keeps `execution_outcome`, `test_assessment`, and\n`claim_category` separate. Claim category is caller-supplied, defaults to\n`unclassified`, and is not inferred from `quality.all_ok`. There is no top-level\nsuccess boolean. Historical `quality` is retained as an assessment declaration,\nnot re-scored or upgraded into a correctness/performance claim.\n\n`evidence_completeness` contains numeric `expected`, `started`, `clean`, and a\n`missing` list of expected test occurrence IDs lacking clean completion. It also\nreports `manifest_present` and `journal_errors`. Clean means a durable per-test\nassessment with exit code zero and explicit `partial: false`; it does not mean\nthe test assessor passed. Interrupted legacy attempts with no per-test assessment\nhave zero established clean tests, even when stdout files exist. Partial evidence\nis never rescored; incomplete assessments are marked `assessed: false` while\nretaining recorded quality. Nine tests started in the interrupted real attempt;\nten completed cleanly in its new attempt. Attempts never share coverage or tokens.\n\n`last_durable_sequence` records physical state-journal line count and the last\nvalid record timestamp. Torn/invalid lines are listed and suppress assessment;\nline count is an observed local-file position, not a remote acknowledgement or\nproof of fsync/host-loss durability. `resource_generation` contains attempt and\nSHA-256 of the owner token, never the raw token; conflicting recorded token hashes\nor attempt/job IDs are refused. `stop_confirmed` requires recorded cleanup\nconfirmation and an explicitly empty owned-container list. A cancellation request\nor terminal journal entry alone cannot confirm stop; no new process probe occurs.\n\n`artifact_refs` lists path + SHA-256 **from export MANIFEST.json**, not recomputed\nhashes of an attempt directory. Default lookup follows `lab/jobs/<job>` to\n`lab/export/<job>/<attempt>/MANIFEST.json`; `--manifest` overrides the locator.\nMissing manifests yield no artifact refs and `manifest_present: false`. These are\nrecorded identities, not a fresh export verification. `bin/lab` exports before\nappending its final terminal event, so an exported state journal may validly have\na different hash/length from the live journal read for `last_durable_sequence`.\nController/evidence on the same host protects against sandbox/process loss only.\n\n## Fixture provenance and verification\n\n`fixtures/envelopes/build-record.json` is an identity-field excerpt of\n`builds/results/b0-baseline-5/build-record.json` (large Buildx provenance omitted).\nThe two jobs' job/owner/result/state files and export manifests are byte-for-byte\ncopies of the requested real records; stdout/stderr payloads are not copied.\n`test-definitions.json` copies relevant current lab definitions as supplemental\ndeclarations, not as retrospective proof of legacy input identity. Tests explicitly\nadd hashes and change the proposed image binding in memory for their accepted\ncase. The synthetic prompt hash in that case tests schema plumbing only.\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -m pytest -q cc/engine_handoff \\\n  --basetemp=cc/engine_handoff/.envelopes-test-tmp \\\n  -o cache_dir=cc/engine_handoff/.pytest_cache\n```\n\n## Questions for the lab-agent team\n\n- What JetPack/L4T, driver/runtime compatibility, and memory accounting/enforcement\n  domains can the Jetson receiver attest to? Fill the template before admission.\n- How will the receiver stage role-bound inputs and independently verify the\n  archive/config/executable identities against delivered bytes?\n- What receiver acknowledgement, owner-generation binding, and stop-confirmation\n  evidence will precede retries and prevent stale result delivery?\n- Which service enforces whole-job wall/capture budgets and GPU concurrency, and\n  what durable destination survives receiver host loss?\n# build_engine\n\nUse this capability to turn a clean, identified local llama.cpp checkout and an optional Git patch into a local, runtime-only CUDA image and an evidence directory. It targets linux/amd64 and CUDA architecture 120 (RTX 5070 Ti). Model weights are supplied separately at runtime.\n\nRequires Python 3.12+ (stdlib only), Git, Docker daemon access, and Docker Buildx/Bake. pytest is needed only for tests. No GPU is needed to compile or collect hashes. The builder installs Ubuntu build packages with apt; an actual build needs cached packages/base layers or repository/registry access. This implementation does not download source, patches, models, or web UI assets. The existing `campaign/cuda-tools:c1` image is not used as the builder because the required builder base is the digest-pinned NVIDIA devel image.\n\n## Run\n\nRun from the campaign directory. Give every attempt a new or empty output directory. For assignment T-N, keep outputs under `cc/build_engine/` or `artifacts/codexN/`. The CLI uses the Dockerfile and Bake recipe in `cc/build_engine/recipes/`; it does not modify or use the older recipes under `builds/llamacpp/`.\n\n```bash\n# Supply the real runtime image digest from trusted/local image evidence.\nexport BASE_RUNTIME='nvidia/cuda:12.8.1-runtime-ubuntu24.04@sha256:<64-hex-digest>'\npython3 cc/build_engine/build_engine.py \\\n  --source ref/llama.cpp --commit 31288c9c6 \\\n  --tag campaign/llamacpp:c1 --out cc/build_engine/results/baseline\n```\n\nThe placeholder must be replaced; it is not a working digest. If BASE_RUNTIME is unset, the CLI inspects the local runtime tag and selects its NVIDIA RepoDigest without pulling it. Missing local images or RepoDigests fail at `base_images`. BASE_DEVEL defaults to the devel digest already recorded in `artifacts/op-309e1f95c716/stdout`; override it with a full `nvidia/cuda:12.8.1-devel-ubuntu24.04@sha256:...` reference when needed. Both bases are validated and supplied as separate name and digest Dockerfile arguments. The CLI never builds from a floating base tag.\n\nTo add a patch, save a unified Git diff to a file and add `--patch path/to/change.patch`. Source HEAD must resolve to `--commit`; tracked changes and untracked files cause rejection. The source checkout remains read-only: Git optional locks are disabled, a `git archive` snapshot is extracted under the output directory, and `git apply --check` runs there before `git apply`. Git discovery stops before the enclosing campaign repository. Binary patches supported by Git are accepted. Submodules are rejected. Git archive honors committed export attributes; ignored and untracked files and `.git` are omitted. Docker ignore files in the staged snapshot are emptied to prevent omission of tracked source. Temporary staging is removed after either result. Keep the supplied patch and original commit for rebuilding; patch bytes are identified by SHA256 but are not retained as a separate output artifact.\n\nThe image contains only the seven requested tools in `/app`, their generated shared libraries and the OpenMP runtime, on top of the CUDA runtime base. It has no compiler, source checkout, or weights. Its entrypoint is cleared, so invoke tools by full path, for example `/app/llama-bench`. Release/shared CUDA compilation uses the flags in `build-record.json`; CPU-native tuning, tests, downloaded UI, and OpenSSL are disabled. `LLAMA_CURL=OFF` is passed as requested, although this checkout treats LLAMA_CURL as deprecated. Source and patch identity are recorded as image labels; binaries' built-in Git version may say `unknown` because `.git` is not staged.\n\n## Engine variants and linkage\n\nUse repeatable `--cmake-define KEY=VALUE` options. Keys must match `^[A-Z0-9_]+$`; values must be nonempty and contain only ASCII letters, digits, or `_./:+,@%=-`. Whitespace, shell metacharacters, empty values, duplicate keys (even with identical values), and all built-in keys are rejected before output creation. This also reserves the built-in compiler-launcher and executable-linker flags. Definitions are sorted by key, passed as the `CMAKE_DEFINES` build argument as space-separated `-DKEY=VALUE` words, and stored in the runtime image label `campaign.cmake.defines` in that same format. The CLI overwrites inherited `CMAKE_DEFINES` environment values.\n\n`recipes` records the SHA256 and size of each recipe file. `recipe_sha256` hashes canonical JSON (sorted keys, compact separators) containing those file identities, all built-in flags, the definitions, and extra targets. Definition values therefore change recipe identity even when recipe bytes are unchanged; reordering definitions does not.\n\nFor the GPU budget shim, build with VMM disabled at compile time. From the campaign root, with a clean source checkout at the shown commit and local base images available:\n\n```bash\npython3 cc/build_engine/build_engine.py \\\n  --source ref/llama.cpp --commit 31288c9c66e59377f7788d920678a565bb89dd37 \\\n  --tag campaign/llamacpp:no-vmm \\\n  --out artifacts/codexN/no-vmm-build \\\n  --cmake-define GGML_CUDA_NO_VMM=ON \\\n  --linkage\n```\n\n`--linkage` enables a final check after saving the image and collecting provenance. The saved OCI or classic Docker archive supplies the symbol evidence: layer contents are streamed with blob and rootfs digest checks, whiteouts are merged, and `/app/libggml-cuda.so` (or a versioned variant) is resolved through its link chain. Only the resolved library is copied into a temporary directory, which is removed after inspection. This follows the streaming/whiteout approach in `cc/engine_handoff/engine_handoff.py`, without extracting the image filesystem.\n\nA pure Python ELF reader (`symbols.tool=\"python-elf\"`, `symbols.location=\"host\"`) reads dynamic symbols and `DT_NEEDED`; neither the host nor runtime image needs readelf or nm. It supports ELF32/64 and both byte orders. ELF files without usable dynamic section headers are explicitly unavailable. A pass requires a found library, a `libcudart.so` or `libcudart.so.*` entry in `DT_NEEDED`, and an undefined `cudaMalloc` import. The record also reports undefined `cudaFree`, `cudaMallocAsync`, `cudaFreeAsync`, `cudaMemGetInfo`, and `cuMemCreate` imports; their absence does not fail the check. Defined symbols and lookalike names do not count.\n\nThe existing in-image `ldd` probe runs with `docker run --rm --network=none --read-only --entrypoint /bin/sh`, the immutable local `run_ref`, a 60-second client timeout, and no GPU. Its output, return code, and `ldd_libcudart_dynamic` remain separate evidence. Missing runtime tools, failed Docker probes, timeouts, or malformed probe output are recorded without changing the host ELF result. This check does not establish that runtime dependencies resolve on the GPU host.\n\nThe record's `linkage` object contains `status` (`not_requested`, `passed`, `failed`, or `unavailable`), `library`, `resolved_library`, config ID, `dt_needed`, `libcudart_dynamic` from `DT_NEEDED`, all six `undefined_imports`, tool return codes/reasons, and output paths. A missing library, invalid archive or ELF, or unreadable dynamic sections is `unavailable`; unavailable measurements are JSON null rather than false.\n\nA failed or unavailable requested check sets top-level `failure_stage=\"linkage\"` and exits 1, while preserving the saved archive and other evidence. `--linkage-optional` enables the same check (even without `--linkage`) but permits a failed/unavailable result with overall success and exit 0; inspect `linkage.status` in that case. With neither flag, linkage is `not_requested`.\n\nThe probe saves `linkage.raw.txt` (framed stdout), `linkage.stderr.txt` (Docker/probe stderr), `linkage.ldd.txt`, and `linkage.symbols.txt` (JSON host ELF evidence). The host evidence is saved even if the Docker probe fails; `linkage.ldd.txt` requires a valid probe response. This ELF check does not prove allocation interception, actual pool selection, or GPU execution; the orchestrator must verify `NO_VMM = 1` and allocation events under the budget shim.\n\n## Outputs and failure handling\n\n`build-record.json` uses schema version 2. Version 1 evidence remains untouched; this capability has no build-record reader. Schema 2 adds `cmake_defines`, `recipe_sha256`, and `linkage`; `cmake_flags` includes all built-in and extra definitions. It records status, failure stage/error, requested and resolved commit, Git tree ID and dirty status, patch SHA256 or null, tag/platform, recipe SHA256 and sizes, base references/digests, Docker and Buildx versions, CMake flags, Buildx metadata, image config ID/RepoDigests/Created/Architecture/Os, tool SHA256 values, saved archive SHA256/bytes, build duration and total duration. Fields remain null or empty when a failure occurs before collection. `build_wall_seconds` measures the Bake invocation, including export; `wall_seconds` covers the whole attempt.\n\nThe other files are:\n\n- `build.stdout` and `build.stderr`: full subprocess stdout/stderr, including compiler output and diagnostics on failure. Binary Git archive output is directed to staging rather than the logs.\n- `buildx-metadata.json`: Buildx metadata, when the build produces it; retained even when the build exits nonzero.\n- `engine-image.docker.tar`: `docker save` of the immutable Docker-reported image ID, collected before runtime probes. This ID may identify an OCI index in the containerd store. Its bytes and SHA256 are recorded after saving.\n\nThe CLI exits 0 only after all required evidence collection succeeds, otherwise nonzero; `--linkage-optional` permits a recorded linkage failure. Failures include source mismatch/dirty tree, unsupported submodules, malformed or inapplicable patch, unavailable Docker/base image, build/export failure, incorrect architecture, missing binaries, save failure, or malformed archive. A failure after building can leave the tagged local image and partial evidence; inspect `failure_stage` and retry in a fresh output directory. Invalid CLI arguments, an unwritable output path, or a nonempty output directory fail before a record can be created. Existing evidence is never overwritten. The CLI does not push, prune, mount the Docker socket in an image, or operate on unrelated containers. A short-lived `docker run --rm --network=none --read-only --entrypoint /bin/sh` container hashes the tools and libraries without requesting a GPU. After resolving the tag once, saving uses the immutable Docker-reported ID. Before hashing or linkage, the archive inspector follows the OCI catalog and nested indexes to the linux/amd64 image manifest and config, skipping other platforms and attestations. It verifies the SHA256 of referenced JSON blobs and requires exactly one matching config. Classic Docker archives are supported by hashing the config bytes named in `manifest.json`. Hashing and optional linkage then use this config digest as the local `sha256:...` Docker image ID; local-only `repo@digest` references are not used. Buildx `containerimage.config.digest` is optional and is cross-checked against the archive when present. Index metadata is cross-checked when the archive retains the index. Missing, ambiguous, or inconsistent archive identities fail before runtime probes. The record keeps `Id`/`docker_reported_id`, `config_digest`, `manifest_digest`, `index_digest`, `index_manifests`, and `docker_id_in_archive_index` separate; an unavailable manifest or index digest is null. `index.json` is the archive catalog and is not assumed to be the original image index.\n\nBake requests maximum provenance and saves metadata separately. This does not imply provenance survives image loading and `docker save`. The archive inspector checks `index.json` and reachable OCI manifests for actual in-toto SLSA provenance statements. A classic Docker archive without an OCI index records `retained: false`; an SBOM or an attestation annotation alone does not count. A save or inspection failure leaves provenance unconfirmed. No signature or attestation trust verification is performed.\n\n## Validation without a build\n\nThe CLI stages its recipes in `.campaign-recipe/` inside the archived source context. Bake receives digest-pinned base references, source and patch identity, extra targets, and extra definitions from the CLI. Prefer the CLI for validation and evidence collection; direct builds using the older `builds/llamacpp/` recipes do not support these variant options.\n\nFrom the campaign directory, run:\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -m pytest -q \\\n  cc/build_engine/test_build_engine.py \\\n  --basetemp=cc/build_engine/.pytest-tmp \\\n  -o cache_dir=cc/build_engine/.pytest_cache\n```\n\nTests use temporary Git repositories and mocked Docker subprocesses. They cover patch rejection, successful patch isolation, successful record schema, build failure logs/metadata, dirty or mismatched source, archive/tool hashing, provenance versus SBOM, and refusing to overwrite evidence. They also cover define validation, variant recipe identity/build arguments/labels, schema 2, captured ELF linkage fixtures, a tiny host-compiled ELF (crafted fallback without cc), OCI layer whiteouts and symlink chains, host symbol inspection independent of image tools, config-ID versus index-ID targeting, and fatal/optional linkage failures. The T-N2 regression uses the real containerd Buildx metadata fixture without config metadata, plus constructed content-addressed OCI index/manifest/config archives (with another platform and an attestation). It checks config metadata mismatches, missing or corrupt configs, ambiguous configs, and index metadata mismatches before any runtime probe. They use no network, GPU, or real engine build.\n\nThis evidence does not prove bitwise reproducibility, runtime correctness, model accuracy, GPU compatibility/performance, or the absence of vulnerabilities. Apt package versions, compiler behavior, timestamps, and upstream build scripts can vary. Base digests and input hashes identify inputs; they do not establish equivalence to an upstream image. The orchestrator must perform the real build and runtime checks. No Docker execution or network access was performed for T-N.\n{\n  \"schema_version\": 2,\n  \"status\": \"success\",\n  \"failure_stage\": null,\n  \"started_at\": \"2026-11-09T22:38:18.571263+00:00\",\n  \"source\": {\n    \"path\": \"/home/muk/projects/siminfeng/campaign/ref/llama.cpp\",\n    \"requested_commit\": \"31288c9c66e59377f7788d920678a565bb89dd37\",\n    \"commit\": \"31288c9c66e59377f7788d920678a565bb89dd37\",\n    \"tree\": \"158a7ade930818efdb06c5561920a8d018d404ba\",\n    \"dirty\": false,\n    \"status_porcelain\": \"\"\n  },\n  \"patch_sha256\": null,\n  \"extra_targets\": [],\n  \"tag\": \"campaign/llamacpp:novmm4\",\n  \"platform\": \"linux/